1) Multi-directional Searching (Line 5-15, Algorithm 4): In this phase, the state xrand obtained from unif ormSample is added to T or any sub-tree within T {τinfo}. If the distance Dist(xrand, T ) is less than λ, xrand is incorporated into T via the Extend function. Similarly, if Dist(xrand, T {τinfo}) < λ, xrand is added to the corresponding sub-tree through AddN ode. Otherwise, a new sub-tree τinfo is generated at xrand via RandomGenerate and subsequently added to T {τinfo} using AddT ree.
2) Heuristic Sampling (Line 16-27, Algorithm 4): During this phase, if two trees are in close proximity, they are merged into a single entity. Two trees (τtree1, τtree2) can be obtained through the tree connection function (meet). If one of these trees (τtree1) is T , the other tree (τtree) serves as heuristic
information to guide T in obtaining the biased sampling state xrand via heuristicSample. Subsequently, a new state xnew can be extended to T via the Extend function. If not, the two neighboring nodes are linked, and all nodes from one sub-tree (τtree2) are integrated into the other sub-tree (τtree1). Heuristic Information Updating
Updating heuristic information manifests in two principal parts (Line 22 and 27, Algorithm 4). Firstly, upon utilization of a sub-tree by T , the corresponding sub-tree τtree is eliminated from the search space via Delete function. This step clears consumed heuristic information, making room for new subtrees. Secondly, when two sub-trees are close, they are merged into a single sub-tree τtree1, and the other sub-tree τtree2 will be removed from the state space using Delete function. This consolidation effectively reduces the computational burden of managing multiple sub-trees, improving the algorithm’s operational efficiency.
